From fb575120cc8cc742e10f1958fa8df01c42fbfbe7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 21:28:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] hw/audio: model the BCM2835 I2S/PCM interface,
 replacing the unimplemented stub

The PCM block was create_unimp(), so a guest could neither configure a
sample rate nor move a frame: reads returned zero and writes vanished.

Model it from the BCM2835 ARM Peripherals datasheet (section 8): both
64-word FIFOs, the frame and channel configuration, the TX and RX
thresholds, the sync flags, the write-one-to-clear error bits, the DMA
request levels and the interrupt, with the serialiser clocked from the
clock manager's pcm mux.

The FIFOs are advanced on demand rather than by a periodic timer at the
frame rate.  A guest may program 48kHz or 3MHz, and a callback per
frame is either wasteful or a storm; instead every register access
first applies however many frame periods have elapsed since the last
one.  A read therefore always reflects the exact state the frame clock
implies, with no dependence on callback latency -- the same reason the
ARM timer in this series computes its counters from the clock.

There is no audio backend: data clocked out of the TX FIFO is
discarded and the RX FIFO fills with zeroes, which is what an
unconnected DIN pin reads on a board.  Everything a driver programs is
modelled.

One deliberate departure from the datasheet, called out in a comment:
its description of TXTHR and RXTHR is defective -- four settings, but
the text for 01 and 10 is identical and omits the fraction ("less than
full", "at least full").  The hardware uses quarters and that is what
is implemented.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
